Administrative staff help with
• recording administration details before, during and after;
• taking enrolments;
• handling general enquiries and correspondence for training;
• maintaining training records;
• coordinating room bookings.
Finance staffs
help with
• costing the training programs;
• maintaining financial records;
• organising payments and reimbursements for
speakers and trainees.
Typist/word processor operators
help with
• producing handouts, exercises;
• photocopying materials for trainers.
Technicians and computer experts help with
• advising on how to use technical equipment;
• setting up any technical equipment before the training sessions;
• operating the equipment;
• attending to problems or maintenance during the program.
Physical resources and maintenance staff
help with
• handling emergency situations (eg air conditioning malfunction, lost keys);
• providing consumables.
Consultants (internal or external) help with
• planning the program;
• costing;
• establishing record keeping systems;
• evaluating your own training skills.
Assessors
help with

• clarifying the learning outcomes, assessment criteria and assessment
conditions;
• suggesting assessment methods trainees will nees to be familiar
with;
• preparing trainees for the assessment process;
• discussing giving trainees feedback about their performance.
Subject experts and guest speakers (internal or external)
help with
• giving demonstrations;
• presenting specialist information.
Seniors in training department
help with
• giving approval of program;
• costing;
• planning before and evaluating during and after the program;
• debriefing after the program.
Other trainers
help with
• running components of the sessions;
• receiving feedback from trainees;
• debriefing after sessions.
Trainees' supervisors, managers or coordinators
help with
• determining training needs;
• providing information on company or industry standards;
• determining trainees' current competencies;
• organising ongoing practice during and after sessions;
• passing on feedback about the program afterwards;
• giving feedback about trainees' improvement in work tasks;
• keeping you in touch with the trainees after the program.
Trainees' co-workers
help with
• giving you feedback about the program that they have heard from the trainees;
• supporting the trainees in their learning and ongoing practice.
